This error occurs because the Room persistence library detected a mismatch between the database schema defined in your Kotlin/Java CustomerEntity class and the actual schema present in the SQLite database file on the device. When you increment your database version, Room executes your migration logic and then performs a validation check. If the "Expected" schema (derived from your code) does not exactly match the "Found" schema (what is actually in the database), this IllegalStateException is thrown. The " Expected" schema indicates that Room expects the customers table to have specific indices and column constraints that are likely missing or differently configured in your existing database. Identifying the Discrepancy Based on the stack trace, Room is looking for two specific indices that seem to be missing or incorrectly defined in the database: idx_customers_business: An index on the businessProfileId column. idx_customers_email: An index on the email column. Additionally, ensure that the notNull properties and affinity (data types) match exactly. For example, if your CustomerEntity marks notes as non-nullable (notNull=true), but the SQL table was created with notes TEXT (which allows nulls), the validation will fail. Solution 1: Update the Migration Script If you are providing a manual Migration object, you must ensure it executes the exact SQL required to reach the expected state. You need to add the missing indices in your migration logic: val MIGRATION_1_2 = object : Migration(1, 2) { override fun migrate(database: SupportSQLiteDatabase) { // Add indices that Room expects database.execSQL("CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS `idx_customers_business` ON `customers` (`business ProfileId`)") database.execSQL("CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS `idx_customers_email` ON `customers` (`email`)") } } Solution 2: Automated Migration If you are using Room's AutoMigration feature (available in Room 2.4.0 +), ensure you have correctly declared the auto-migration in your @Database annotation. If the auto-migration is failing, it usually means there is an ambiguous change (like a renamed column) that requires a @RenameColumn or @DeleteColumn specification via a Spec class. Solution 3: Synchronizing Entity with SQL If the indices were added to your code by mistake or are not actually needed, remove the @Index annotations from your CustomerEntity. Room generates the "Expected" schema based on these annotations: @Entity( tableName = "customers", indices = [ Index(value = ["businessProfileId"], name = "idx_customers_business"), Index(value = ["email"], name = "idx_customers_email") ] ) data class CustomerEntity(...) Quick Fix for Development If you are in the early stages of development and do not care about preserving existing data on the emulator, you can use .fallbackToDestructiveMigration() when building your Room database. This will wipe the database and recreate it with the correct schema whenever a version mismatch is detected, preventing this exception: Room.databaseBuilder(context , AppDatabase::class.java, "bizap-db") .fallbackToDestructiveMigration() .build()

