### You said: This error occurs because the schema defined

This error occurs because the schema defined in your Room CustomerEntity class does not match the actual schema of the customers table in the SQLite database after a migration was executed. Room performs a validation check by comparing the "Expected" schema (derived from your Kotlin/Java code) with the "Found" schema (the actual state of the database file). If there is even a minor discrepancy in column nullability, data types, or indices, Room throws this exception to prevent data corruption. Root Cause Analysis The logs show that Room expected a specific table structure including columns like createdAt, address, notes, and indices like idx _customers_business, but the migration script you provided to the RoomDatabase.Builder failed to transform the existing table into that exact state. Common reasons include: Nullability Mismatch: You defined a field as non-nullable (notNull=true) in your Entity, but the SQL migration script created the column as nullable, or vice versa. Missing Indices: You added @Index annotations to your Entity but forgot to execute the CREATE INDEX commands in your Migration object. Default Values: There is a discrepancy between the defaultValue property in the Entity and the one defined in the SQL ALTER TABLE statement. Type Affinity: A column expected to be INTEGER was created as TEXT (or another type) in the manual SQL script. How to Fix the Migration To resolve this, you must ensure the SQL commands in your Migration class produce a table that exactly matches the "Expected" output in the error log. Locate your Migration: Find the code where you define val MIGRATION_X_Y = object : Migration(X, Y) { ... }. Verify SQL Commands: Check the migrate function. For the customers table, ensure you have statements covering all new columns and indices. For example: database.execSQL("ALTER TABLE customers ADD COLUMN notes TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT ''") database.execSQL("CREATE INDEX IF NOT EXISTS idx_ customers_business ON customers(businessProfileId)") Compare Constraints: Pay close attention to notNull=true. If your Entity has val notes: String, the SQL must include NOT NULL. If it is val notes: String?, the SQL must allow nulls. Using Automated Migrations Since you are on a modern version of Room, you can often avoid manual SQL errors by using AutoMigrations. If you are only adding columns or creating new tables, Room can generate the migration for you. Update your Database class: @Database( entities = [Customer Entity::class], version = 2, // The new version autoMigrations = [ AutoMigration(from = 1, to = 2) ] ) abstract class AppDatabase : RoomDatabase() { ... } Destructive Re-creation (Development Only) If you are in the early stages of development and do not care about preserving existing local data on the emulator, you can avoid writing migrations entirely by allowing Room to recreate the tables from scratch. Add fallbackToDestructiveMigration() to your database builder: Room.databaseBuilder( context, AppDatabase::class.java, "database-name") .fallbackToDestructiveMigration() .build() Note: This will wipe all user data in the database whenever the version number is incremented without a matching migration path.

